// Research metadata for the currently-shipped clause templates in
// `clauseTemplates.js`, keyed by clause id. Generated from
// `lease-clauses.csv` (repo root, now an 8-state pass -- CO, WY, KS, NE, MN,
// ND, SD, OH) -- that CSV, plus each state's own
// `lease-clause-decision-log-<STATE>.md` (Ohio's is a pair: `-OH.md` and
// `-OH-extend-manifest.md`) and `lease-clause-decision-log-architecture-
// review.md`, is the actual source of truth; this file is a compiled,
// code-readable copy of five of its columns (`rule_type`, `content_type`,
// `verification_status`, `notes`, plus the `effective_from`/`last_checked`
// pair added during the architecture-review session).
//
// Deliberately NOT merged onto the clause objects in `clauseTemplates.js`:
// that file's objects are spread directly into the `GET /api/clause-templates`
// response, and none of this is meant to be UI-facing. This file exists so
// future tooling (e.g. an automated check that no rent-related clause
// implies a rent increase more than once per 12 months, or that no clause
// anywhere shifts attorney fees to only one party) has real structured data
// to run against, without that data leaking to the frontend.
//
// `ruleTypes` is an array (not a single value) because a clause can
// legitimately be more than one type at once -- e.g. Use of Security Deposit
// is both REQUIRED (must exist) and PROHIBITED (bars charging for
// wear-and-tear/preexisting conditions) in the same clause. Values:
// REQUIRED / CONDITIONAL / PROHIBITED / CONSTRAINED / RECOMMENDED -- see
// `lease-clause-decision-log-CO.md` §3 for what each means.
//
// `effectiveFrom`/`lastChecked` are per-CSV-ROW, not per-state -- a clause
// tagged with several states can only hold one date pair even though each
// state tag was actually verified on a different real date. See the
// architecture-review log §5 for the full limitation and its known
// consequence (a shared clause silently correcting for one state's re-audit
// without every other tagged state's log being told). Either field may be
// blank for a row the CSV never backfilled a date for.
//
// The 64 CO-only rows whose verification_status/effective_from/last_checked
// were moved out of `lease-clauses.csv` into `lease-clause-citations-CO.csv`
// during the Sep 2026 citation-extraction project (see CLAUDE.md's Known
// Issues) are backfilled from that citations file at generation time, only
// when the main CSV's own field is blank -- this generator run is the first
// to actually do that; a prior regeneration without the backfill would have
// (and briefly did, mid-session, before being caught and fixed) shipped
// those 64 rows with blank metadata instead of their real values.
//
// **2026-09-24 (California):** each entry also carries `topicKey`, from the
// CSV's new `topic_key` column -- one key per topic cluster across states
// (normalizing id drift like `landlords-access` vs `landlord-entry-ca`), so a
// cross-state comparison is a group-by instead of an id-guessing exercise
// (lease-clause-decision-log-CA.md §5.40). Regenerated from the 9-state CSV.
//
// `contentType` is always `LEASE_CLAUSE` here, since only LEASE_CLAUSE
// items from the research ever make it into `clauseTemplates.js` in the
// first place -- LANDLORD_EDUCATION findings from the same research live in
// `landlordEducation.js` instead, and OUT_OF_SCOPE findings live only in the
// decision logs, not in shipped code.
//
// Only clauses currently shipped in `clauseTemplates.js` (`is_active: TRUE`
// LEASE_CLAUSE rows) appear here. Rows the research explicitly deactivated
// (`is_active: FALSE` -- an unselected posture variant, a clause later
// superseded to blank `states`, or content pulled after a correction) keep
// their metadata in the CSV/decision logs only.

