    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself EDITED this session | NE extended (session 6, closing the last open NE item): Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1416(2) permits applying the deposit 'to the payment of rent and the amount of damages which the landlord has suffered by reason of the tenant's noncompliance with the rental agreement or section 76-1421' — substantively the same rent-plus-damages-beyond-wear-and-tear framing this clause already uses. No last-month's-rent restriction like Kansas's, so no override needed. Not extended to WY in this pass — WY currently has no clause of this type at all (security-deposit-use-wy doesn't exist), a separate, pre-existing gap noticed while resolving this, not fixed here since it's outside this session's Nebraska-specific scope. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): matches Minn. Stat. § 504B.178, subd. 3(b) — landlord may withhold only for rent defaults and to restore the property, ordinary wear and tear excepted. No conflict. | ND extended (North Dakota session 1, statute walk): N.D.C.C. § 47-16-07.1(3) permits application to pet/negligence damage, unpaid rent, and cleaning/repair costs necessary to restore the property, reasonable wear and tear excepted — compatible with this clause's existing self-limiting language. No ND-specific override needed. | SD extended (South Dakota session 1, whole-library generic-clause audit): SDCL 43-32-6.1's deposit-application concept (deposit secures performance of the rental agreement) is compatible with the generic's existing self-limiting language (default/damage beyond wear-and-tear, not preexisting conditions); no SD-specific override needed for the use/application language itself. Return mechanics are SD-specific and handled separately by security-deposit-return-sd. | WY ADDED 2026-08-28 (deposit split, Taylor's decision): WY previously folded application language into security-deposit-return-wy. Split to the CO pattern. Compatible with Wyo. Stat. 1-21-1208(a). TWO DELIBERATE GIVEAWAYS accepted: (1) cleaning deductions only if 'substantially less clean', vs 1208(a)'s 'clean to the condition at start'; (2) 1208(a)'s catch-all 'any other costs this Lease provides for' not carried over. Both more tenant-favorable than WY requires.",
