  "lead-based-paint": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-21",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-19",
    topicKey: "lead-based-paint",
    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE extended (session 2, whole-library generic-clause audit): generic mechanics, no NE-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with NE findings from this project. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): generic mechanics, already vetted against KS/NE's prohibited-lease-provisions statutes (the strictest bar found in this project to date) since this clause was already tagged KS;NE; no MN-specific statutory language or number identified in this session's statute walk that conflicts. No exculpatory/liability-waiver language present. | PART 5 PRIMARY-TEXT READ 2026-08-27 (Taylor supplied full C.R.S. 38-12-503): MATERIAL CORRECTION (federal spot-check). 40 CFR 745.113(b)(1) requires the Lead Warning Statement to appear VERBATIM in the lease; the prior clause paraphrased it, which does not satisfy the regulation. Replaced with the exact regulatory text (federal regulatory text is a government edict, not copyrighted). Also added the other elements 745.113(b) requires: landlord's affirmative disclosure or statement of no knowledge (b)(2), the records/reports list or statement of none (b)(3), tenant's acknowledgment of receipt (b)(4), and the accuracy certification (b)(6). Exposure for noncompliance is meaningful: 42 U.S.C. 4852d(b)(5) statutory penalty inflation-adjusted to $22,263 per violation (24 CFR 30.65(b)), plus treble damages and fees. NOTE: the 10-day inspection opportunity is a SALE requirement and correctly absent here. NOTE ALSO: this clause is shared across CO;WY;KS;NE;MN - the fix is federal and uniform, so it benefits all five, but the other four states' logs should record that this clause changed. Retention: keep records 3 years (40 CFR 745.113(c)). | 5a.1 PROPAGATION NOTE -> KS (recorded 2026-08-29, KS re-audit). The Lead Warning Statement was corrected to verbatim per 40 CFR 745.113(b)(1) during a prior state's session. This is a UNIFORM FEDERAL requirement with no Kansas-specific variation, so Kansas inherits the corrected text safely and no KS-specific review of the change is owed. Kansas has no state lead disclosure statute layering additional requirements on top of the federal rule. last_checked reset per 5a.1. | ND AND SD ADDED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit, checklist-to-CSV reconciliation screen). HOW THIS WAS MISSED AND WHY IT MATTERS: this row is REQUIRED / LEASE_CLAUSE, and the states field is the display source of truth, so a Steinoak-generated ND or SD lease for pre-1978 target housing was omitting the federally mandated Lead Warning Statement ENTIRELY. Exposure per this row's own record: 42 U.S.C. 4852d(b)(5) statutory penalty inflation-adjusted to $22,263 per violation (24 CFR 30.65(b)), plus treble damages and fees. The consolidated checklist asserted 'Present (federal)' for ND. That assertion was true about the LAW and false about the LIBRARY -- federal law applying to ND is not the same as the clause being tagged ND. Both ND's original session and this re-audit's checklist fold-in recorded 'Present (federal)' without anyone verifying a tagged row existed. SD carried the identical defect. ROOT CAUSE: ND's original whole-library generic-clause audit worked through Rent & Payment, Security Deposit, maintenance, Pets, Access & Entry and Default & Termination, but never walked the Disclosures group systematically. A generic-clause audit that is not exhaustive over GROUPS will silently skip whole categories. 5a.1 JUDGMENT: UNIFORM, safe to inherit. The requirement is federal (40 CFR 745.113(b); 42 U.S.C. 4852d) with no state-specific variation, exactly as recorded for the KS propagation. Neither ND nor SD layers a state lead-disclosure statute on top -- ND's ch. 47-16 was read in full this session and contains none, and ND's disclosure position is that the move-in condition statement is its only state-required disclosure. SD's absence of a state layer is ASSUMED from the parallel posture and must be confirmed in the SD session, which is next. 5a.1 PROPAGATION NOTES OWED to CO, WY, KS, NE, MN logs: the states field changed, clause TEXT did not. No re-review is owed on the existing five tags. CA: extended to California 2026-09-19 after re-screen against the full CA pass (the extend manifest had deferred tagging until the blocked set was resolved, and the step was then missed). No CA conflict found.",
  },
