  "assistance-animal-accommodation": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-21",
    lastChecked: "2026-08-31",
    topicKey: "assistance-animal-accommodation",
    notes: "NEW - universal/federal baseline (Fair Housing Act), CO tag added since the CO-specific override below was verified against it this session WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE extended (session 2, whole-library generic-clause audit): generic mechanics, no NE-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with NE findings from this project. | Display-collision bug fixed (session 7, full-library supersedes audit): CO,WY removed — already covered by a state-specific override that supersedes this row; both versions were displaying simultaneously before this fix. | AUDIT 2026-08-27 (Opus/high/research re-audit): FEDERAL CURRENCY FLAG - HUD withdrew FHEO-2020-01 (the assistance-animal guidance) effective 2025-09-17 (Fed. Reg. Docket FR-6571-N-01); HUD states it 'should not be relied upon.' The clause text does not cite FHEO-2020-01 and rests on the Fair Housing Act itself (42 U.S.C. 3601 et seq.; 24 CFR 100.204), which is unchanged - so the operative language still stands. Flagged NEEDS_REVIEW rather than corrected because the federal posture is in flux (including post-Loper Bright litigation over the ESA no-fee rule) and any HUD replacement guidance should be picked up. CO-specific layer (documentation limits, misrepresentation warning) is unaffected. | ESA/FEDERAL-DIVERGENCE RESOLUTION 2026-08-27: REMAINS NEEDS_REVIEW - deliberately, and now for a sharper reason. This is the generic/universal version resting on the federal FHA baseline alone, with no state overlay. On May 22 2026 HUD's FHEO rescinded the 2020 assistance-animal notice and adopted a new enforcement posture: it will find reasonable cause only for animals individually trained to provide disability-related assistance (the ADA service-animal standard), and will no longer expect housing providers to categorically grant accommodations or fee waivers for untrained assistance animals (ESAs). HUD has stated it intends notice-and-comment rulemaking. Clause text UNCHANGED and should stay unchanged: this is an enforcement posture, not a change to 42 U.S.C. 3601 et seq. or 24 CFR 100.204, it does not bind courts, private FHA plaintiffs, or DOJ, and narrowing the clause would create asymmetric risk (an uncollected pet fee vs. a discrimination claim). Holding NEEDS_REVIEW because any state adopting this clause without its own ESA protection inherits a genuinely unsettled federal picture - each new state must confirm its own basis the way CO just did. | 5a.1 PROPAGATION NOTE -> KS (recorded 2026-08-29, KS re-audit), covering the earlier move to NEEDS_REVIEW following HUD's guidance withdrawal. RULE GAP FLAGGED, NOT AMENDED: 5a.1 triggers on edits to bodyText, rule_type or content_type. A verification_status change is NOT on that list, so this propagation note is owed by instruction and practice, not by the rule as written. Same species as the row-vs-text gap flagged at WY log 3. | KS DETERMINATION COMPLETE, ROW DOES NOT MOVE. Kansas splits by animal type: trained service animals have a strong independent state basis (K.S.A. 39-1102/1107/1108, statutory no-fee rule, criminal backing at 39-1103, class C misdemeanor); ESAs are expressly excluded from that statute by 39-1113(f), leaving only the generic KAAD duty at 44-1016(h)(3)(B), which is textually independent of federal law but judicially untested in housing. See edu-kaad-accommodation-duty-ks and edu-service-animal-denial-penalty-ks. | TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS THE ROW STAYS NEEDS_REVIEW: (1) the row is tagged KS;NE and NEBRASKA HAS NOT BEEN CHECKED - a KS-only answer cannot resolve a shared row; (2) even for KS alone the answer is split, with an untested generic basis running against an adverse express exclusion in a neighboring statute. | DRAFTING ISSUE FOR A FUTURE PASS, not resolved here: the row's documentation sentence says Landlord may request reliable documentation \"to the extent permitted by applicable law.\" Kansas's statutory verification procedure (K.S.A. 39-1111) does NOT reach housing, so for KS that phrase resolves against federal law alone. | UNRESOLVED DATE CONFLICT: project records treat 2025-09-17 (guidance withdrawal) and 2026-05-22 (narrowed enforcement) as two sequential events; secondary sources located during the KS re-audit treat them as conflicting accounts of one event. This row's NEEDS_REVIEW rationale cites both. Must be reconciled against HUD primary documents before this row is resolved for any state. | NE RE-AUDIT 2026-08-31: NE removed — Nebraska has its own express service-animal HOUSING statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§20-131.01 to 20-131.04, read from primary source) with a no-additional-deposit rule this generic does not state, so NE now takes assistance-animal-accommodation-ne. Both blockers on this row's NEEDS_REVIEW are now resolved: (1) the NE state-law basis is determined; (2) the HUD question is resolved as TWO SEQUENTIAL EVENTS, not conflicting accounts of one — 2025-09-17 withdrawal of FHEO-2020-01 and FHEO 2013-01 (later formalized at Fed. Reg. Docket FR-6571-N-01), then a separate 2026-05-22 FHEO enforcement memo narrowing enforcement to individually-trained animals. Neither touched the FHA statute itself. Row flipped to VERIFIED for Kansas. §5a.1: states-field change only, bodyText untouched — no propagation note owed on clause text, but a note IS owed in the KS log recording the status flip.",
  },
