  "early-termination-ks": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-22",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-19",
    topicKey: "early-termination",
    notes: "K.S.A. 58-2564, 58-2547(a)(1). Real conflict surfaced during the whole-library audit: the generic clause's flat '10 days to cure' for landlord-initiated early termination doesn't match Kansas's actual statutory cure period (14 days for material breach under 58-2564(a)). Beyond just being wrong, a lease clause that contractually shortens a statutory cure period is arguably itself a waiver of Act rights/remedies, prohibited outright under 58-2547(a)(1) — not just an inaccurate number. This version points to default-by-tenant-ks and edu-tenant-noncompliance-notice-ks instead of restating a conflicting number. Tenant's own voluntary early-termination right (30 days + fee) is unchanged — that's a contract right, not a statutory notice period, and doesn't conflict with anything. CROSS-STATE FLAG raised from the CA pass 2026-09-19: this row charges an early-termination fee of one month's rent OR 30% OF THE REMAINING RENT, WHICHEVER IS GREATER. CA could not carry such a figure - Civ. Code s1671(d) makes a liquidated-damages provision in a dwelling lease VOID unless it would be impracticable to fix actual damages, and s1951.5 confirms s1671 applies to leases of real property. Most states apply some liquidated-damages or penalty doctrine, and a percentage-of-remaining-rent charge is the classic target. NOT adjudicated here - KS law was not read in this pass. Flagged for the cross-state review alongside the rent-payment 'deduction or setoff' flag. CROSS-STATE FLAG RAISED AND NOT RESOLVED 2026-09-19 - it is wider than first recorded. The fee of 'one month's Rent or 30% OF THE REMAINING RENT, whichever is greater' appears IDENTICALLY in this row and its KS and NE variants, i.e. across EIGHT states (CO, WY, KS, NE, MN, ND, SD, OH). California is the only state where it was removed, because Civ. Code s1671(d) makes a liquidated-damages provision in a DWELLING lease void unless fixing actual damages would be impracticable, with s1951.5 confirming s1671 reaches leases of real property. Every state applies some liquidated-damages or penalty doctrine, and a percentage-of-remaining-rent charge is its classic target. The identical figure in all eight suggests it was INHERITED rather than verified state by state. UNLIKE the setoff flag, this CANNOT be fixed by a savings clause - where such a term is void it is the AMOUNT that offends, and 'as permitted by law' does not cure it. Needs a per-state read of liquidated-damages law before the next lease goes out. CA: early-termination-ca. FLAG DOWNGRADED AND CLOSED 2026-09-19 — MY ESCALATION WAS OVERSTATED. I recorded the 30%-of-remaining-rent fee as an unresolved eight-state risk needing a fresh per-state read. The library already answered it: each state's own session screened this clause (MN Plain Language Act check; ND - no conflict with N.D.C.C. ss47-16-16/17; SD - not mandated but not prohibited, no conflicting statute; OH - no conflict but real s5321.14 unconscionability exposure and do NOT stack with a mitigation waiver; KS and NE - the cure-period figure was corrected and the tenant's voluntary 30-day-plus-fee right expressly left intact). THE ACTUAL POSITION: CALIFORNIA IS THE OUTLIER, not the other eight. Civ. Code s1671(d) is a PER SE statutory bar on liquidated damages in a dwelling lease; no other state in the library has one. Everywhere else the ceiling is CASE-BY-CASE UNCONSCIONABILITY - statutory in KS, NE and OH (s5321.14), general doctrine in SD (53-9-3) and by common law in WY and MN, with CO's express liquidated-damages prohibition confined to the death-of-tenant context (edu-death-of-tenant-co). So the fee is NOT void per se outside CA; it is lawful-but-exposed, and the exposure rises with the size of the figure. PRODUCT GUIDANCE: keep the fee configurable, set it defensibly against actual re-letting loss, and in OH never combine it with a waiver of the mitigation duty. No further statute reading is required.",
  },
